1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
Give RAG-Fusion a better reranker,

2
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:05,610
and you'd expect fusion to matter less.

3
00:00:06,080 --> 00:00:07,670
We found the opposite.

4
00:00:07,720 --> 00:00:09,520
Its lift doubled.

5
00:00:10,050 --> 00:00:12,080
Same fifty documents.

6
00:00:12,590 --> 00:00:13,900
Four different judges.

7
00:00:13,950 --> 00:00:15,920
This is how the rerankers in the RAG-Fusion

8
00:00:15,970 --> 00:00:18,360
experiment decide what goes on top.

9
00:00:19,550 --> 00:00:21,560
Every method has the same shape.

10
00:00:22,330 --> 00:00:24,030
Retrieval pulls a pool of fifty candidates

11
00:00:24,030 --> 00:00:25,180
for the query.

12
00:00:25,230 --> 00:00:27,160
A reranker rescores that pool.

13
00:00:27,750 --> 00:00:29,630
Only the top five reach the model that

14
00:00:29,630 --> 00:00:30,880
writes the answer.

15
00:00:30,930 --> 00:00:33,270
So the reranker decides what the answer is

16
00:00:33,270 --> 00:00:34,700
allowed to see.

17
00:00:34,750 --> 00:00:37,910
Three of the rerankers are cross-encoders:

18
00:00:38,390 --> 00:00:41,880
MiniLM, bge-base, and bge-large.

19
00:00:41,930 --> 00:00:45,230
A cross-encoder reads the query and one

20
00:00:45,230 --> 00:00:47,540
document together, as a single passage,

21
00:00:47,770 --> 00:00:49,400
and outputs one number.

22
00:00:49,450 --> 00:00:51,360
It does that fifty times,

23
00:00:51,730 --> 00:00:53,160
once per document.

24
00:00:53,210 --> 00:00:55,470
The numbers only mean something relative to

25
00:00:55,470 --> 00:00:57,730
each other, so all you can do is sort.

26
00:00:57,730 --> 00:01:00,350
The three differ mostly in size:

27
00:01:00,350 --> 00:01:02,730
thirty-three million, two hundred and

28
00:01:02,730 --> 00:01:04,920
seventy-eight million, and five hundred and

29
00:01:04,920 --> 00:01:06,720
sixty million parameters.

30
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:08,910
Jev works differently.

31
00:01:08,960 --> 00:01:10,700
It's a decision model, not a trained

32
00:01:10,700 --> 00:01:11,810
reranker.

33
00:01:11,860 --> 00:01:13,920
The query and up to thirty documents go in

34
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:15,720
together, as one state.

35
00:01:16,120 --> 00:01:18,200
Jev then answers one yes-or-no question per

36
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:20,010
document, all in parallel:

37
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:22,200
is this document relevant?

38
00:01:22,320 --> 00:01:24,220
Each answer is a calibrated probability.

39
00:01:24,220 --> 00:01:26,480
Point nine seven means relevant.

40
00:01:26,480 --> 00:01:28,950
Point zero one means it isn't.

41
00:01:29,340 --> 00:01:30,450
Because they're probabilities,

42
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:32,110
you can threshold them,

43
00:01:32,160 --> 00:01:33,410
not just sort them.

44
00:01:33,460 --> 00:01:34,490
One catch:

45
00:01:34,540 --> 00:01:37,060
ask twice, and the probabilities wobble by a

46
00:01:37,060 --> 00:01:38,350
few hundredths.

47
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:41,230
RAG-Fusion changes what goes into the pool.

48
00:01:41,740 --> 00:01:44,130
The original query, plus four rewrites,

49
00:01:44,360 --> 00:01:46,830
each search with keywords and with vectors.

50
00:01:47,340 --> 00:01:49,140
That's ten ranked lists.

51
00:01:49,540 --> 00:01:51,530
Reciprocal rank fusion merges them.

52
00:01:52,140 --> 00:01:54,780
Each document earns one over sixty plus its

53
00:01:54,780 --> 00:01:57,060
rank, summed across every list it appears

54
00:01:57,060 --> 00:01:58,230
in.

55
00:01:58,280 --> 00:01:59,820
Documents that keep turning up,

56
00:01:59,820 --> 00:02:01,400
rise.

57
00:02:01,450 --> 00:02:04,100
Where the reranker sits matters too.

58
00:02:04,310 --> 00:02:06,110
Our pipeline fuses first,

59
00:02:06,290 --> 00:02:08,260
then reranks the merged pool once.

60
00:02:08,810 --> 00:02:11,050
The paper we replicated reranks each list

61
00:02:11,050 --> 00:02:13,840
separately, then fuses the reranked lists.

62
00:02:14,530 --> 00:02:16,690
Same reranker, different place in the

63
00:02:16,690 --> 00:02:17,950
pipeline.

64
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:20,460
Here's what that looks like on two hundred

65
00:02:20,460 --> 00:02:22,260
NFCorpus queries.

66
00:02:22,420 --> 00:02:24,840
On its own, Jev is the strongest reranker:

67
00:02:24,840 --> 00:02:28,190
point three eight N D C G at ten,

68
00:02:28,380 --> 00:02:31,340
against point three three for bge-large.

69
00:02:32,160 --> 00:02:34,120
Fusing first, then reranking,

70
00:02:34,480 --> 00:02:37,300
lifts both bge models by about point zero

71
00:02:37,300 --> 00:02:40,030
two five, and the lift doubles with Jev,

72
00:02:40,180 --> 00:02:41,980
to plus point zero five,

73
00:02:42,060 --> 00:02:43,220
in two separate runs.

74
00:02:43,220 --> 00:02:46,670
Reranking each list first does the opposite.

75
00:02:47,180 --> 00:02:48,980
It helps most with bge-base,

76
00:02:49,040 --> 00:02:51,150
and stops helping with Jev.

77
00:02:52,750 --> 00:02:54,620
So what does each option cost?

78
00:02:55,130 --> 00:02:56,860
These timings are from this laptop,

79
00:02:56,910 --> 00:02:58,700
so read them with care.

80
00:02:58,750 --> 00:03:00,740
Fusion's real price is the rewrite call:

81
00:03:01,070 --> 00:03:02,150
about one and a half seconds,

82
00:03:02,150 --> 00:03:04,350
and around five cents per thousand queries

83
00:03:04,370 --> 00:03:06,260
using a small language model.

84
00:03:06,510 --> 00:03:08,850
The cross-encoders are slow here only

85
00:03:08,850 --> 00:03:10,650
because they're running on a laptop.

86
00:03:10,730 --> 00:03:13,480
On a server GPU they'd take milliseconds,

87
00:03:13,630 --> 00:03:15,880
but then you're paying to host that GPU.

88
00:03:16,710 --> 00:03:18,240
Jev is hosted for you:

89
00:03:18,290 --> 00:03:19,930
about a third of a second including the

90
00:03:19,930 --> 00:03:21,850
network, and about a dollar per thousand

91
00:03:21,850 --> 00:03:23,120
queries.

92
00:03:23,170 --> 00:03:26,010
On quality alone, Jev by itself already

93
00:03:26,010 --> 00:03:28,080
beats bge-large with fusion.

94
00:03:28,830 --> 00:03:30,930
Add fusion, and it's the best result we

95
00:03:30,930 --> 00:03:31,920
measured.

96
00:03:31,970 --> 00:03:34,060
The query router saves a little time,

97
00:03:34,310 --> 00:03:36,110
but with a rewrite model this cheap,

98
00:03:36,330 --> 00:03:37,900
it doesn't save money.

99
00:03:37,950 --> 00:03:40,250
Here's the plain version of why adding Jev

100
00:03:40,250 --> 00:03:42,050
made fusion work better.

101
00:03:42,490 --> 00:03:44,290
Fusion is a wider net.

102
00:03:44,610 --> 00:03:46,060
Across these two hundred queries,

103
00:03:46,110 --> 00:03:47,950
it puts about twelve percent more of the

104
00:03:47,950 --> 00:03:50,120
right documents into the pool of fifty.

105
00:03:50,210 --> 00:03:52,340
The reranker is the sorter:

106
00:03:52,750 --> 00:03:54,830
it decides which ten of those fifty the

107
00:03:54,830 --> 00:03:56,400
answer gets to see.

108
00:03:56,450 --> 00:03:59,470
The cross-encoders move a quarter or less of

109
00:03:59,470 --> 00:04:01,910
fusion's extra finds into the top ten.

110
00:04:02,210 --> 00:04:04,010
Jev moves more than half.

111
00:04:04,190 --> 00:04:06,610
My guess is that fusion's extra finds are

112
00:04:06,610 --> 00:04:08,710
the harder ones, because they came from

113
00:04:08,710 --> 00:04:10,450
rewrites and don't look much like the

114
00:04:10,450 --> 00:04:13,390
original query, and Jev is better at

115
00:04:13,390 --> 00:04:14,540
recognising them.

116
00:04:14,590 --> 00:04:17,270
A wider net only pays off if whoever's

117
00:04:17,270 --> 00:04:18,910
sorting the catch can tell what's worth

118
00:04:18,910 --> 00:04:20,200
keeping.

119
00:04:20,250 --> 00:04:23,090
A stronger reranker didn't make fusion

120
00:04:23,090 --> 00:04:24,160
redundant.

121
00:04:24,210 --> 00:04:26,380
It gave it more to work with.

122
00:04:26,590 --> 00:04:28,370
Putting Jev inside the fusion step,

123
00:04:28,370 --> 00:04:30,450
to weight, route, or gate it,

124
00:04:30,450 --> 00:04:31,920
didn't pay.

125
00:04:31,970 --> 00:04:34,650
The reranker washes out anything that only

126
00:04:34,650 --> 00:04:36,450
changes the pool.

